  "sections": {
    "what": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "text": "Orlistat is a weight-loss drug that acts in the gut. It is sold by prescription as Xenical (120 mg) and over the counter as Alli (60 mg).",
        "src": [
          {
            "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=6240792b-9224-2d10-e053-2a91aa0a2c3e",
            "label": "Xenical label, DailyMed",
            "date": "published Jan 2026"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=a2d3bd73-f3af-4ea5-a57c-66b0004cfe4f",
            "label": "Alli Drug Facts label, DailyMed",
            "date": "published Mar 2026"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": "h3",
        "text": "How it acts, according to the FDA label"
      },
      {
        "t": "list",
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Orlistat binds to and switches off lipases, the enzymes in the stomach and small intestine that break down dietary fat. Undigested fat is not absorbed and passes out in the stool. This lowers the calories taken in.",
            "src": [
              {
                "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=6240792b-9224-2d10-e053-2a91aa0a2c3e",
                "label": "Xenical label, DailyMed",
                "date": "published Jan 2026"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Because it acts in the gut, its main side effects are bowel changes, and it can lower absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.",
            "src": [
              {
                "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=6240792b-9224-2d10-e053-2a91aa0a2c3e",
                "label": "Xenical label, DailyMed",
                "date": "published Jan 2026"
              },
              {
                "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=a2d3bd73-f3af-4ea5-a57c-66b0004cfe4f",
                "label": "Alli Drug Facts label, DailyMed",
                "date": "published Mar 2026"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "research": [
      {
        "t": "lead",
        "text": "Orlistat has randomized trials lasting up to 4 years. In them, people on orlistat plus diet lost a few kilograms more than people on diet alone, and many people did not finish."
      },
      {
        "t": "h3",
        "text": "Human studies"
      },
      {
        "t": "study",
        "meta": "Europe · 1998 · Randomized trial",
        "title": "Orlistat for weight loss and regain, 743 adults, 2 years (Sjöström et al.)",
        "facts": [
          {
            "k": "Design",
            "v": "randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial at 15 European centres"
          },
          {
            "k": "People",
            "v": "743 obese adults (BMI 28 to 47) on a calorie-deficit diet; 688 entered treatment"
          },
          {
            "k": "Length",
            "v": "4-week lead-in, then 1 year of weight loss and 1 year of maintenance"
          },
          {
            "k": "What was given",
            "v": "orlistat 120 mg 3 times a day or placebo"
          },
          {
            "k": "Measured",
            "v": "weight change; cholesterol; glucose; side effects"
          }
        ],
        "found": "In year 1, people lost 10.2% of body weight on orlistat and 6.1% on placebo. In year 2, those who stayed on orlistat regained about half as much as those switched to placebo. Gut side effects were more common on orlistat.",
        "src": [
          {
            "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9683204/",
            "label": "Sjöström L, et al. Lancet 1998",
            "date": "published Jul 1998"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": "study",
        "meta": "Sweden · 2004 · Randomized trial",
        "title": "Orlistat to prevent type 2 diabetes, 3,305 adults, 4 years (XENDOS)",
        "facts": [
          {
            "k": "Design",
            "v": "randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial; all had lifestyle changes"
          },
          {
            "k": "People",
            "v": "3,305 adults with BMI 30 or more; 21% had impaired glucose tolerance"
          },
          {
            "k": "Length",
            "v": "4 years"
          },
          {
            "k": "What was given",
            "v": "orlistat 120 mg or placebo 3 times a day"
          },
          {
            "k": "Measured",
            "v": "time to type 2 diabetes; weight change"
          }
        ],
        "found": "52% on orlistat and 34% on placebo completed treatment. Diabetes developed in 6.2% on orlistat and 9.0% on placebo; the difference was seen only in people with impaired glucose tolerance. Mean weight loss after 4 years was 5.8 kg vs 3.0 kg.",
        "src": [
          {
            "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14693982/",
            "label": "Torgerson JS, et al. Diabetes Care 2004 (XENDOS)",
            "date": "published Jan 2004"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "us": [
      {
        "t": "lead",
        "text": "Orlistat is FDA approved. Xenical 120 mg (1999) is prescription-only, for obesity with a reduced-calorie diet. Alli 60 mg (2007) is sold over the counter to overweight adults. As of November 1, 2026."
      },
